The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}g(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-x^3+4x}{4x^3-2x^2+4}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-x^3+4x}{x^{3}}}{\dfrac{4x^3-2x^2+4}{x^{3}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{-1\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}+\dfrac{4\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x^2}}{\dfrac{4\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}-\dfrac{2\cancel{x^2}}{x\cdot\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{4}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-1+\dfrac{4}{x^2}}{4-\dfrac{2}{x}+\dfrac{4}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-1+0}{4-0+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{4}
\end{align}$
